This is an unofficial project to create a free-content manual for 
'OpenLoco', a free and open-source transport simulation derived from 
'Chris Sawyer's Locomotion'.


Rationale

'Chris Sawyer's Locomotion', as with practially all games of its time, 
comes with a manual. While this manual is mostly applicable to 
'OpenLoco', it cannot be freely redistributed or modified, does not 
document changes made by 'OpenLoco', and is not fully suitable as a 
digital document.

'Chris Sawyer's Locmotion's manual is proprietary, like the orignal 
game. Thus, if the community manages to develop a complete set of free 
sounds, graphics and scenarios for use with 'OpenLoco', players may be 
able to play OpenLoco, but may struggle to understand how to play.

That also means that futures new to 'OpenLoco' have not be clearly 
documented anywhere. These include cloning vehicles, holding the build 
piece button to build multiple segments and the ability to repaint 
individual components of vehicles. A new manual can document these 
changes in one place.

An additional limitation of the 'Chris Sawyer's Locomotion' manual is 
that it was meant for use as a printed document. Modern retailers like 
GOG and Steam provide a PDF version. However, this is less accessible 
and readable than a digital-native book would be.

This project thus aims to:

  1. Provide a free-content manual. This means that anyone can use, 
     share and modify it as they please, so long as they follow the 
     licence conditions.

  2. Document 'OpenLoco' as it exists, including its many improvements 
     over 'Chris Sawyer's Locomotion'.

  3. Be readily available in digital formats, such as HTML.


Non-goals

This project does not aim to:

  - Document 'Chris Sawyer's Locomotion'.

  - Compare 'OpenLoco' with any other game, including 'Chris Sawyer's 
    Locomotion'.


Building

The book is built using mdBook, version 0.5.2, which is available from: 
https://github.com/rust-lang/mdBook. It also uses MathJax: some chapters 
will need the browser to support JavaScript.


Style Notes

Keep blank lines between elements in the source. This includes list 
items. It makes it much easier to format paragraphs.

Limit lines in the source to 80 characters.

I find GitHub-style tables annoying to work with and would rather use 
embedded HTML.

Use Australian English: 'colour' instead of 'color', 'railway' instead 
of 'railroad', 'truck' instead of 'lorry', etc.

Try to avoid duplicating information. For example, don't discuss the 
exact details of cargo acceptance in both the towns and stations 
section.

Link between topics as approrpriate.


